        </div>
        <div class="cell">
          <b>Flat price. Not a cut of your sales.</b>Your bill doesn't grow just because you had a good
          month.
        </div>
        <div class="cell">
          <b>Every number double-checked.</b>Against Shopify and your ad platforms, before you see it.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- PROBLEM -->
    <section class="problem" id="problem">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="eyebrow" style="color:var(--gl-green-accent)">Why this exists</div>
        <h2>Right now, nobody can tell you whether <em>last month worked</em>.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          You have the tools. You have the numbers. What you don't have is one place where they
          agree — and one person who has actually read them.
        </p>

        <div class="problems">
          <div class="pcard">
            <div class="n">01</div>
            <h3>Shopify shows sales, not profit</h3>
            <p>
              It knows what you sold. It doesn't know what your products cost you, what you paid in
              ad spend, or what you gave away in discounts. So it can't tell you what you kept.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="pcard">
            <div class="n">02</div>
            <h3>Every ad platform marks its own homework</h3>
            <p>
              Meta says it made you money. Google says the same. Add up what they each claim and you
              get more sales than you actually had. Both can't be right.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="pcard">
            <div class="n">03</div>
            <h3>Dashboards don't tell you what to do</h3>
            <p>
              A screen full of charts is not an answer. Somebody still has to look at them, work out
              what changed, and decide what to do differently this month.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- WHAT YOU GET -->
    <section id="what">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="eyebrow">What you get</div>
        <h2>Four answers you can't get anywhere else today.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          No jargon required. If you can read a bank statement, you can read this report.
        </p>

        <div class="gets">
          <div class="get">
            <div class="idx">01</div>
            <div>
              <h3>What you actually kept</h3>
              <p>
                Your sales, minus what the products cost you, minus discounts, returns, shipping,
                payment fees and every rupee of ad spend. The number at the bottom of the page, not
                the one at the top.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="figure">
              <div class="v good">$41,280</div>
              <div class="k">left after everything</div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="get">
            <div class="idx">02</div>
            <div>
              <h3>Whether your ads paid for themselves</h3>
              <p>
                One honest number: for every $1 you spent on ads, how many dollars came back. Not
                Meta's version. Not Google's version. The real one, using your total sales and your
                total ad spend.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="figure">
              <div class="v">3.24&times;</div>
              <div class="k">you break even at 2.10&times;</div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="get">
            <div class="idx">03</div>
            <div>
              <h3>What a new customer costs you</h3>
              <p>
                And whether their first order covers that cost, or whether you're paying to acquire
                people who never come back. This is the difference between growing and just getting
                busier.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="figure">
              <div class="v">$38.40</div>
              <div class="k">first order brings back $46.20</div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="get">
            <div class="idx">04</div>
            <div>
              <h3>Where the numbers disagree</h3>
              <p>
                What each ad platform claims it delivered, next to what your store actually recorded.
                When those two drift apart, that's the finding — and it usually means you're setting
                budgets on numbers that are too optimistic.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="figure">
              <div class="v bad">40%</div>
              <div class="k">gap on Meta, last month</div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

